s without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a time problem with numbers such as 11 and 40, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Adding minutes without rolling 60 minutes into an hour invents times like 11:90","Use the same time method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter","Retype the worked model numbers 11 and 40 as the answer"],"question":"Solve a fresh time problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Adding minutes without rolling 60 minutes into an hour invents times like 11:90","Time problems combine reading clocks with elapsed time, schedules and simple planning constraints","12:30","Label units when comparing 11 with 40"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for time?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for time, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses time with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for time; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about time, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with time, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Adding minutes without rolling 60 minutes into an hour invents times like 11:90.. Correction uses 12:30.. Why: restores accurate time.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w30-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise time.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use time with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify problem and explain why it matters.","I can practise schedule independently on a new prompt.","I can notice elapsed needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Time problems combine reading clocks with elapsed time, schedules and simple planning constraints. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Time problems combine reading clocks with elapsed time, schedules and simple planning constraints","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about time with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nFilm 1 h 25 min starting 3:50 → ends 5:15. If you must leave by 5:00, the film is too long.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA lesson starts at 11:40 and lasts 50 minutes. When does it end?\n\nFEEDBACK\n12:30.","explain":"Time problems combine reading clocks with elapsed time, schedules and simple planning constraints.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing d